      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, if your test confirms a nitrogen deficiency, you should look for a granular fertilizer where the first number on the bag is significantly higher than the subsequent two, such as a 36-0-0 or 24-12-12 blend. A high-nitrogen fertilizer will push your grass back to a lush, deep green shade relatively quickly, often within 10 to 14 days, but it can compromise root depth if overused long-term. In contrast, a balanced fertilizer prevents this rapid surface growth and instead focuses on strengthening the roots over several weeks, leading to a tougher lawn that withstands Portland's variable weather better in the long run.",
